  "result": "The gate returned **OK** on the first run, using one of the six allowed runs. It reported 19 witnesses, 24 names and no composed rules. Both files are written and `task_result.md` is in the working directory.\n\nEarly on I also tried a second shell command that wasn't the gate command. The sandbox denied it and it never ran, so it cost nothing from the budget.\n\n**The rule** (`NRS_484B_450_1l.lean`):\n- **Who it applies to:** the subject is a vehicle (`DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`).\n- **When it applies:** some other vehicle `b` is at rest, and its body is within `p.edge_standing_gap` of a road edge that lies on a highway. The subject is side by side with `b`, and `b` and that edge are on the same side of the subject.\n- **What it requires:** the subject is not at rest. It may stop if either:\n  - another member of traffic is ahead of it in the same lane, within `p.queue_halt_gap`; or\n  - a lane it occupies is governed by a stop sign, a yield sign, a red signal, an active railway signal or gate, or a police officer signalling stop.\n\n**Choices the node left open:**\n- The devices in s5 (\"compliance with law\") are a subset of those in s7 (\"traffic-control device\"). I wrote each device once in a single list rather than repeating them; the meaning is the same.\n- \"A red arrow\" covers every arrow direction, since the node names none.\n- I added no narrowings of my own.\n\n**Witnesses:** the eight planned scenes all reach their expected verdicts. I added 11 more (`branch_5`–`branch_15`) so every part of the rule is tested. They cover the queue exception (2 m ahead allows the stop, 12 m doesn't) and each device or officer exception, along with a case where each one doesn't apply:\n- a stop sign governing only the other lane;\n- a flagger instead of a police officer;\n- a police officer signalling proceed;\n- a green signal.",
